---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ContactInvitation from './ContactInvitation.astro';
import Icon from './Icon.astro';
import EditorialModel from './EditorialModel.astro';
import EditorialStudy from './EditorialStudy.astro';
import StructuredEditorial from './StructuredEditorial.astro';
import {getPageEditorial} from '../data/content';
interface Props {draft?:boolean;}
const {draft=false}=Astro.props;
const editorial=await getPageEditorial('approach',draft);
---
<Layout noindex={draft} title='Approach' description='How NRTH frames a question, investigates a direction and chooses a practical next step across its venture portfolio.'>
 {draft&&<aside class='preview-banner'><a href='/preview'>Editorial workspace</a><span data-preview-status>Draft preview</span></aside>}
 <section class='page-heading is-dark'><span class='section-label'>Our approach</span><h1>Ask carefully.<br/><em>Make deliberately.</em></h1><p>A broad imagination is useful. A specific question gives it somewhere to go. Make the uncertainty visible, examine it with the right method and let the evidence shape the next step.</p><a class='inline-link' href='#working-model'>Explore the working model</a></section>
 <div id='working-model'><EditorialModel kind='approach'/></div>
 <EditorialStudy kind='approach'/>
 <section class='editorial-split section-dark'><div data-enter><span class='section-label'>Begin with the work</span><h2>What needs<br/>to change?</h2></div><div data-enter><p>Describe the decision, the task or the experience in concrete terms. Find the person dealing with it, what they know at that moment and the constraints that make the next step difficult.</p><p>“Improve transport” leaves too much open. “Understand how an updated arrival time reaches the person planning the next handoff” gives an investigation a specific task, audience and boundary.</p><div class='editorial-callout'><h3>Make the question observable.</h3><p>Look for a situation you can describe, a person you can learn from and a decision that an answer could change. Use an anonymized example when the context involves private or commercially sensitive information.</p></div><a class='inline-link' href='/research'>Build a focused research outline</a></div></section>
 <section class='editorial-intro-grid' aria-label='Criteria for choosing a next step'>
  <article data-enter><h3>Useful to someone.</h3><p>Connect the idea to a person, a task and a reason that improving it would matter. A striking concept still needs a practical context.</p><ul><li>Who encounters the problem?</li><li>Which decision or experience changes?</li><li>What happens if nothing changes?</li></ul></article>
  <article data-enter><h3>Grounded in evidence.</h3><p>State the observation, its source and the limits of what it can tell you. Match the strength of the conclusion to the work actually completed.</p><ul><li>What has been observed?</li><li>Which assumptions remain open?</li><li>What result would change the direction?</li></ul></article>
  <article data-enter><h3>Clear enough to act.</h3><p>Give a proposed next step boundaries, responsibilities and a way to review the result. Include the surrounding dependencies.</p><ul><li>What is included in the scope?</li><li>Who needs to contribute or review it?</li><li>How will the next decision be made?</li></ul></article>
 </section>
 <section class='editorial-split'><div data-enter><span class='section-label'>Choose a practical step</span><h2>Set the direction.<br/>Define the edges.</h2></div><div data-enter><p>Information access, technical dependencies, operational timing and specialist review can determine whether a promising direction is practical. Bring them into the conversation early.</p><p>An exploratory conversation, a study and a product engagement have different expectations. Deliverables, responsibilities, timing and commercial arrangements need to be established for the particular opportunity.</p><p>The same care applies after a decision: keep the purpose understandable, make changes visible and preserve the reasons behind the direction.</p><a class='inline-link' href='/partnerships'>Shape a partnership introduction</a></div></section>
 <section class='editorial-split'><div><span class='section-label'>Before a conversation</span><h2>Start where<br/>you are.</h2></div><div class='disclosure-list'><details open><summary>Is an early idea enough?<Icon name='plus'/></summary><p>Yes. Describe the context, the question and the change you have in mind. A short example of the work today is usually more useful than a polished presentation. Separate observations from the outcome you hope to reach.</p></details><details><summary>Can a question cross fields?<Icon name='plus'/></summary><p>Many do. Describe the whole situation and the perspectives it needs. A vehicle signal might connect to a transport decision, an information boundary and a logistics handoff. The conversation can establish which direction belongs at the centre of the question.</p></details><details><summary>What should I bring?<Icon name='plus'/></summary><p>The people involved, a concrete example, the constraints that matter and any evidence already available. Include important dependencies and timing. Share only material you are entitled to provide; private records or sensitive details are not needed to introduce a question.</p></details><details><summary>Does an experiment establish readiness?<Icon name='plus'/></summary><p>An experiment answers a bounded question. It does not by itself establish that a complete product, clinical application, integration or material is ready for use. Evidence, testing and appropriate review depend on the intended application.</p></details></div></section>
 <StructuredEditorial editorial={editorial} id='approach-detail' heading='The method, in more detail.'/>
 <ContactInvitation headline='Where would you begin?'/>
</Layout>
